<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.2.6: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02102</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.2.6: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>297442a2-da46-4ce2-8d26-9298e45ec184</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Help students reflect on the key ideas of the lesson by discussing, as a class or with a partner, questions such as the following:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item>“What are some limitations of solving \(-2x^2 − 2x + 40 = 10\) by guessing and checking? What about by graphing?” (Sample answer: If the solution is not an integer, then it will be difficult to find the solution. Graphing will also not produce an exact solution, only an estimate.)</item>
  <item>“Which equation do you think is easier to solve: \(-2x^2 − 2x + 40 = 0\) or \((8 − 2x)(x + 5) = 0\)? Why?” (Sample answer: The equation in factored form is easier to solve since the equation is set equal to 0. The zeros, or solutions, are easier to identify.)</item>
  <item>“Which appears easier to solve: \((8 − 2x)(x + 5) = 10\) or \(-2x^2 − 2x + 40 = 10\)? Why?” (Sample answer: They are both difficult to solve. The factors in the factored form are not equal to zero on their own. The equation \(-2x^2 − 2x + 40 = 10\) is one step closer to solving mathematically, if we can figure out a way to factor it.)</item>
</list></section>
</content>
